{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"CA","level":"state","category":"application_screening_fees","status":"in_force","title":"California tenant screening fee cap and conditions (Civ. Code § 1950.6)","requirement":"Landlords may charge a screening fee only to obtain information about a prospective tenant, such as a consumer credit report, and only up to the annual maximum. The applicant should receive a copy of their credit report within 7 days of the landlord receiving it, a receipt for the fee, an itemized list of out-of-pocket expenses and time spent on screening, and a refund of any unused portion of the fee. If the applicant provides a reusable screening report, the landlord must use it. No screening fee may be charged if no rental unit is actually available. The landlord must return the fee to any applicant not selected, or have a policy of reviewing applications in the order received (first qualified applicant gets the unit) and not charging a fee to any applicant not considered.","key_value":"Maximum tenant screening fee for 2026: $68.96","coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants applying for rental units in California; the page applies it to Berkeley rentals.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"The landlord cannot charge a prospective tenant a screening fee if no rental unit is actually available. The landlord must also return a screening fee to any applicant not selected, or have a policy under which they: (1) review applications in the order received and the first qualified applicant gets the unit, and (2) do not charge a screening fee to any applicant not considered.","supporting_spans":["Landlords may use screening fees to obtain information about a prospective tenant like a consumer credit report."],"confidence":0.88,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","level":"city","category":"application_screening_fees","status":"in_force","title":"Berkeley Municipal Code Chapter 13.78: screening fee disclosure and ban on non-refundable fees for existing tenancies","requirement":"When an owner charges a fee to purchase a consumer credit report and to validate, review, or otherwise process an application, the owner must provide, in the rental application or in a separate disclosure before receiving the fee, a clear and conspicuous Tenant Screening Fee Rights Statement and a statement of the maximum fee cap under Civ. Code § 1950.6(b) (BMC 13.78.010). Owners may not charge an existing tenant a non-refundable fee to renew a tenancy, in whole or in part, including any fee tied to a roommate's departure or a request to add or replace a roommate (BMC 13.78.016). Chapter 13.78 applies to all residential rental agreements regardless of contrary lease language, and any lease provision that violates it is unenforceable (BMC 13.78.018).","key_value":"No non-refundable fee to existing tenants for renewal or roommate changes; mandatory screening fee rights statement and fee cap disclosure","coverage_conditions":"Owners of residential rental property in Berkeley and their agents; applies to all residential rental agreements.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"BMC 13.78","quoted_span":"An owner of residential rental property (or the owner’s agent) cannot charge a non-refundable fee to any existing tenant for the purpose of renewing a tenancy, in whole or in part, including any fee associated with the departure of a roommate or to request to add or replace a roommate in a pre-existing household.","supporting_spans":["Chapter 13.78 applies to all residential rental agreements regardless of any contractual language in any rental agreement or lease to the contrary."],"confidence":0.85,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"The page is about application and screening fees only. Rules for the other five categories are not stated. Enactment and effective dates are not given, so they are null. The page states the status as current. BMC 13.78.010, .016 and .018 are folded into one Berkeley record because they are part of the same chapter and category."}
